What locking strategy would you use? One global lock or fine-grained locks?

For a standard LRU Cache, I'd choose a global lock
Because every get() and put() can modify the same DLL.



Why not fine-grained locking?
You could lock individual keys, but the DLL is global.

Example:

Thread A → get(1) → move Node 1 to HEAD
Thread B → get(2) → move Node 2 to HEAD

Both operations modify the same HEAD pointers. So you'd still need a lock around the DLL, which makes fine-grained locking much more complicated.



One advanced improvement: If extremely high concurrency is required, I'd consider sharding the 
cache into multiple independent LRU caches, each with its own lock. This allows operations on different shards to proceed concurrently



1. Sharded LRU Cache — Single Machine
              LRU Cache
                  |
        ┌─────────┼─────────┐
        ↓         ↓         ↓
     Shard 1   Shard 2   Shard 3
      Lock 1    Lock 2    Lock 3

*************    All shards are still inside one application/server/JVM. *********

The goal is to improve concurrency:

Thread A → Shard 1 → Lock 1
Thread B → Shard 2 → Lock 2

        ↓
Can execute concurrently

This is still a local in-memory cache.


We are just sharding on single machine.
Not Distributed cache kindof...



